11) Correctness, Security, Maintainability, Test Coverage, DX Audit
Correctness
- Core flow works for happy path; correctness depends on LLM prompt compliance.
- No guaranteed schema-driven query construction; risk of LLM generating invalid SQL.

Security
- No auth; webhook and SSE endpoints are open.
- CORS is permissive; combined with lack of auth raises exposure.

Maintainability
- Clear small codebase; single-file backend but lacks structured models.
- Hardcoded logic for call_id storage; no separation of concerns.

Test Coverage
- No tests in repo.
- test_e2e.sh is helpful but not automated and requires ngrok + live VAPI.

Developer Experience
- E2E script is good; however, missing frontend/.env example will block onboarding.
- No README with quickstart or architecture diagram.

12) Code Smells / Architectural Risks
- In-memory state used as a substitute for session management; not future-proof.
- Implicit trust of external tool-calls without verification.
- Tool handlers return concatenated text strings; brittle for LLM parsing and front-end reliability.
- No separation of transport from business logic in backend; hard to test or extend.

